@@ -8,6 +8,36 @@ This is the source code of [Fizzy](https://fizzy.do/), the Kanban tracking tool
 You are welcome -- and encouraged -- to modify Fizzy to your liking.
 Please see our [Development guide](docs/development.md) for how to get Fizzy set up for local development.
 
+## Deploying on one server
+
+The included [Kamal configuration](config/deploy.yml) runs the web app, SQLite databases, file storage, and Solid Queue background jobs in one container. [kamal-proxy](https://kamal-deploy.org/) terminates SSL with an automatically renewed Let's Encrypt certificate.
+
+1. Prepare a Linux server with Docker. Point your hostname's DNS at it, allow inbound TCP ports 80 and 443, and make sure you can SSH to it as `root`. If you use another SSH user, add an `ssh: user:` setting to `config/deploy.yml`.
+2. Replace every `fizzy.example.com`, `smtp.example.com`, and example email address in `config/deploy.yml`. Adjust the SMTP port, authentication, and STARTTLS setting for your mail provider. The `SMTP_DOMAIN` value is the hostname sent by the client in `HELO`/`EHLO`.
+3. On the computer from which you deploy, create an ignored file containing the secrets. Keep this file safe and reuse the same `SECRET_KEY_BASE` for future deploys:
+
+   ```bash
+   umask 077
+   printf 'SECRET_KEY_BASE=%s\n' "$(openssl rand -hex 64)" > .env.deploy
+   cat >> .env.deploy <<'EOF'
+   SMTP_USERNAME='replace-me'
+   SMTP_PASSWORD='replace-me'
+   EOF
+   ```
+
+4. Load the secrets and deploy:
+
+   ```bash
+   set -a
+   source .env.deploy
+   set +a
+   bin/kamal setup
+   ```
+
+Kamal uses a local registry tunneled over SSH, so no hosted container registry account is needed. Docker must also be available on the deploying computer. The default build targets `amd64`; change `builder.arch` if the server uses another architecture.
+
+The named Docker volume `fizzy_storage` is mounted at `/rails/storage`. It contains all production SQLite databases and uploaded files, and is retained when a new application container is deployed. Back up this volume regularly. Normal updates with `bin/kamal deploy` preserve it; the data remains until the `fizzy_storage` Docker volume is explicitly deleted.
+
 
 ## Contributing
 
diff --git a/config/deploy.yml b/config/deploy.yml
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..087220a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/config/deploy.yml
@@ -0,0 +1,51 @@
+# One-box Fizzy deployment. Replace the example host and mail settings below,
+# export the secrets listed in .kamal/secrets, then run `bin/kamal setup`.
+service: fizzy
+image: fizzy
+
+servers:
+  web:
+    - fizzy.example.com
+
+proxy:
+  ssl: true
+  host: fizzy.example.com
+
+# Kamal runs this registry on the deploying computer and tunnels image pulls
+# over SSH, so a registry account is not required.
+registry:
+  server: localhost:5555
+
+builder:
+  arch: amd64
+
+# SQLite databases and local file uploads all live below /rails/storage.
+volumes:
+  - fizzy_storage:/rails/storage
+
+asset_path: /rails/public/assets
+
+env:
+  clear:
+    RAILS_ENV: production
+    APP_HOST: fizzy.example.com
+    ACTIVE_STORAGE_SERVICE: local
+
+    # OSS Fizzy normally runs Solid Queue in Puma. Keep this explicit so jobs
+    # and recurring tasks run without adding a second container.
+    SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA: true
+
+    MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS: "Fizzy <fizzy@example.com>"
+    SMTP_ADDRESS: smtp.example.com
+    SMTP_PORT: 587
+    SMTP_DOMAIN: fizzy.example.com
+    SMTP_AUTHENTICATION: plain
+    SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO: true
+  secret:
+    - SECRET_KEY_BASE
+    - SMTP_USERNAME
+    - SMTP_PASSWORD
+
+aliases:
+  console: app exec --interactive --reuse "bin/rails console"
+  shell: app exec --interactive --reuse "bash"
